<TopicPage slug="subjects/math/advanced/integration">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Integration is calculus's other half: where the derivative measures instantaneous change, the
    <strong>integral accumulates</strong> — adding up infinitely many infinitely thin slices.
    Areas, volumes, distance from speed, total charge from current: any "sum of continuously
    varying stuff" question is an integral. The discovery that makes it work: the accumulation
    function A(x) = ∫f has <strong>derivative f</strong> — the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus,
    which turns hard sums into anti-derivatives.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="area under x² accumulates as x³/3 — the red curve is the running total of the blue one's area" widget="plotter" config={intCfg} />

  <p>
    At x = 1 the area under the parabola is ⅓; at x = 2 it's 8/3; at x = 3 it's 9. The red curve
    is that running total — and its slope at any point equals the height of the blue curve
    there. Slope and area are the same fact seen from two sides.
  </p>

  <h2>The working technique</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Power rule reversed:</strong> ∫xⁿ dx = xⁿ⁺¹/(n+1) + C. The +C matters — anti-derivatives come in vertical families, and boundary conditions pick the member.</li>
    <li><strong>Definite integrals:</strong> ∫ᵃᵇ f = F(b) − F(a) — the Fundamental Theorem converting accumulation into subtraction.</li>
    <li><strong>Substitution</strong> is the chain rule reversed; <strong>integration by parts</strong> is the product rule reversed. Most of a first course is recognising which undo-move fits.</li>
    <li><strong>When symbols fail, slices still work:</strong> numerical integration (Simpson's rule) computes areas to any accuracy — how physics software integrates motion it can't solve by hand.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The derivative lesson showed x² begetting 2x; this page runs the film backwards. Together
    they are the complete loop that Newton and Leibniz opened — and the differential-equations
    lesson next door is where the loop becomes physics.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> OpenStax, Calculus Volume 1 (Integration); Stewart, Calculus.</p>
</TopicPage>
